I'm joined today by dr. Melanie joy who  is a harvard-educated psychologist and  author of the book why we love dogs eat  pigs and where cows she is also  president of beyond carnism where which  you can learn more about at carnism dot  org it's so great to talk to you I think  there's so many ways we could enter this  conversation and I'm going to do it in a  way that sort of will resonate with our  audience because if things I've talked  about the title of your book why we love  dogs and eat pigs I once decided that  upon spending some time around pigs and  learning about their intelligence and  really how they are similar to dogs in  many ways I decided I wasn't going to be  eating pigs anymore and I haven't eaten  pork in in many years and some people  when I tell them that it sort of makes  sense to them intuitively and others say  David that just it sounds so arbitrary  it doesn't really make any sense to me  and that arbitrariness is the first  thing I want to talk to you about  because as you've spoken about before  there are many different ways in which  human decisions about diet and eating  animals are sort of inherently arbitrary  and and I guess the question is are some  of those decisions more or less  arbitrary than others or is this just  inherent to human nature and decisions  about diets well first of all thanks for  having me on today I'm really looking  forward to talking about this issue with  you and an answer to your question it's  really important to recognize how our  decisions are made in the first place or  what the what forces are shaping our  attitudes and feelings and behaviors  when it comes to the decisions that we  make especially the decisions that have  a profound impact on others such as  whether to eat others or not or whether  to eat animals or not and my work  focuses on what I have referred to as  carnism this is the focus of my research  the invisible belief system or ideology  that conditions us to eat certain  animals so carnism is a it's a dominant  belief system so it's really woven  through the very structure of society to  shape norms laws the beliefs behavior  cetera and it becomes internalized  shaping the very way we think and feel  about eating animals and so really what  carnism teaches us to do is to classify  certain animals as edible and all the  rest is inedible and in meat-eating  cultures around the world people learn  to classify only a tiny handful of  animals as edible all the rest they  perceive as inedible and often even  offensive as most disgusting too soon  and so what does this tell us  some people will hear this and they'll  say well it sounds almost like like  you're you're referring to religious  doctrine people might think about halal  or kashrut guidelines that existed in  Islam and Judaism respectively but  you're really going well beyond that I  mean some of these guidelines may be for  some informed by religion but really  you're also talking about non-religious  sort of socio cultural norms right  exactly what I'm talking about is  ideology and more specifically an  irrational ideology it's important to  recognize that you know most of us share  the same core human values values such  as compassion and justice and what  systems such as carnism do that are  organized around violence you know meat  cannot be procured without violence and  egg and dairy production caused  extensive harm to animals  so most people would not willingly cause  this harm to animals especially or  animals suffering especially when the  suffering is so intensive and so  completely unnecessary and so what  carnism does is it distorts it uses a  set of Defense's that distort our  perceptions and numb our feelings so  that we act against our values without  fully realizing what we're doing and I  think there's something interesting  they're not not to interrupt but I think  there's something interesting and I want  to dig in a little bit to this idea of  most people like you said would not go  around willingly causing harm to others  and in this case where you're sort of  referring to the the animal industrial  complex for lack of a better term  might this not apply in many ways in  life I mean as a sort of just particular  example you could point out to people  who drive to work listen you're driving  to work is not good for the environment  do you realize that and you could also  make the case that none of those  individuals are willingly and  deliberately saying I want to harm the  environment and thus I am going to drive  to work so there must be something  beyond this right I mean is it is it  just this idea of rationalization or is  it the idea maybe of a greater good by  being at work I am doing more good maybe  than I am doing by polluting the  environment with my car and maybe  there's a sort of similar type of  internal irrational rationalized logic  that people might use for meat  consumption yeah I mean that's a great  point you know sometimes we do need to  maybe act against our values in order to  support the greater good but when it  comes to eating animals the vast  majority of us have completely  unexamined choices and when we look at  the facts we look at what the data tells  us eating animals causes not only the  the tremendous suffering and deaths of  77 billion land animals globally every  year it also causes harm to our bodies  as eating animal products isn't linked  with some of the leading causes of  disease believing diseases in the  developed world today  it causes extensive harm to the  environment there is no rational  argument today to my knowledge that one  could make that eating animals is in any  way in service of the greater good what  we try to do it beyond carnism is really  get people to you know we're not  advocating advocating telling people  what they should and shouldn't eat what  we're advocating is awareness we believe  that people need and deserve to know the  truth not simply about animal  agriculture and its consequences but out  about carnism and how it shapes our  perceptions so that we check our  critical thinking at the door when we  make some of the most frequent and  important choices that we do make in our  daily lives and with awareness people  can make their choices freely because  without awareness there is no free  choice I know that person  you're vegan and in much of what I've  seen you write and say about this issue  you talk both about sort of the  repercussions of eating meat but you  also talk about it as you and I have  been speaking about this sort of  arbitrary double standard that exists  with many animals that people choose to  eat or not to eat I I'd love to for you  to sort of break down a little bit how  important the double standard angle is  to you I mean if someone said it you  know I don't care about animals I  believe that objectively speaking this  isn't my view but if someone said I  believe animals are lesser than humans  and I don't care about animal suffering  I'm totally consistent I will eat any  animal is that someone that to you is at  least morally consistent and do you have  how much how much would you respect that  still in the context of your broader  views about environmental impact etc I'm  really less concerned with moral  consistency and more concerned with  people acting in alignment with their  natural empathy and practicing  compassion so that we can have the kind  of world that we most of us actually do  want to have so you know most people do  in fact care about animals we know for  example that humans and and some other  animals are actually hardwired to  empathize with others empathy is our  natural state and what systems such as  carnism do is they cause us to simply  disconnect from our natural empathy  toward those species we've learned to  classify as edible so what I think is  most important is not looking at simply  which animals we do eat which animals we  don't eat but really asking this  question how can I be more connected  with my authentic thoughts and feelings  so that I can make choices that are in  my own best interest and in the interest  the best interest of others there's been  pretty extensive writing about asserting  that were it not for eating meat much  longer ago in human history humans may  have died out right and to differing  degrees I'm sort of simplifying it but  this case has been made to differing  degrees in terms of meat as a portion of  a as one component  of the human diet what's your sense of  number one do you believe that  technology and sort of the modern world  now allow someone to not consume any  animal products and still be able to  live a normal life in society and if you  believe that at some point that was  necessary the consumption of animal  protein was necessary for the for the  survival of Homo sapiens when do you  think that that changed in other words  at what point did we come to dominate  agriculture and and our environment in  such a way that eating meat was no  longer necessary for survival  yeah I mean what I believe in terms of  you know whether eating animals is  healthy or not is significantly less  relevant in my opinion than what the  data show you know but the data I want  to make sure before you go I want to  make sure that we that I'm clear on my  question because I what my question was  do you believe that at some point eating  meat in the history of Homo sapiens was  indispensable and if so at what point  did we start to be able to get away from  me in a way that you believed it was no  longer necessary for our survival yeah I  mean I'm not a historian so I can't give  you hard facts on this what I do know is  that anthropologists say that our very  earliest ancestors were actually fruit  eaters like some other non-human  primates today are we evolved to eating  small amounts of meat because we did  have to and it's it's you know it's it's  clear that humans did at some point in  history eat other animals and they did  this in order to survive yeah but we  know that in the history of  vegetarianism ethical vegetarianism at  least is traced back to about 3500 years  3,500 years ago were the first  suggestions of the first I should say  people who chose to practice  vegetarianism for ethical and at that  time it was religious reasons we do have  a vast amount of literature today  research demonstrating that a  plant-based or vegan diet is not only a  healthy diet but can be beneficial in  the prevention and even treatment of  disease  so today there are some people in the  world who eat animals because they have  to they're geographically or  economically unable to make their food  choices freely but you know when a  behavior becomes a choice it takes on an  ethical dimension that it didn't have in  quite the same way before and for many  people in the world today they don't eat  animals because they have to they eat  animals because it's just the way things  are because they've been conditioned to  and not to recognize conditioned not to  recognize that there is an alternative  there is another way to be well  significant food for thought it is  fascinating and we will talk about of  course the book why we love dogs eat  pigs and wear cows and the ideas  presented in it in the future as well  we've been speaking with the book's  author dr. Melanie joy thanks so much  for talking to me about this thank you  so much for having me
 

